Andy Johnson <usenet@REMOVETHIS.swervy.tk> wrote in news:cngqfh$jqk$1
@joe.rice.edu:

> Joe Tebelskis wrote:
> 
>> In a recent game I encountered and defeated my own ghost from an
>> earlier game.  Is it considered unethical to refer to my notes from
>> the end of that game to fully identify the items left at my own grave?
>> 
>> Joe Tebelskis
> 
> I would be one of the people who calls it very unethical. It's kind of 
> like savescumming; you're giving yourself an advantage outside of what 
> the game provides. Now, if you happen to remember you were carrying a 
> dozen scrolls of teleport, go ahead and use that knowledge. But I think 
> it's wrong to intentionally look up the life of another adventurer to 
> determine what they were carrying.

I sort of agree in that this is what I do (not look anything up but I 
tend to remember what the interesting things in the bones file were) but 
I don't really think this is logical. If it's logical to use my memory 
then why isn't it logical to use memory aids (notes)?  After all, it's 
not like either one is provided in game.  Certainly this reincarnation of 
N should not know what things the other N died with.  I think that one 
Should pretend not to even know what the other character died with if one 
is going to follow this argument, but I don't turn down advantages I'm 
offered, so I don't.


